---
// /become-a-driving-instructor/adi-part-2/ (issue #85).
import InstructorGuideLayout from '../../components/guide/InstructorGuideLayout.astro';
import { FEES, GOVUK } from '../../data/instructor-guide';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the ADI part 2 test?',
    answer: 'ADI part 2 is the driving ability test, the second of the three ADI qualifying tests. It takes around one hour and covers an eyesight check, 5 vehicle safety questions, general driving ability, 2 manoeuvres and about 20 minutes of independent driving. You can book it once you have passed part 1.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many faults can you have in the ADI part 2 test?',
    answer: 'You pass with no more than six driving faults and no serious or dangerous faults. A driving fault is not potentially dangerous, but repeating it could make it a serious fault. A serious fault is something potentially dangerous, and a dangerous fault involves actual danger.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many attempts do I get at ADI part 2?',
    answer: 'Three. The GOV.UK test page says a failed eyesight test counts as one of them. After a third fail you must retake and pass ADI part 1 again, and wait 2 years from when you first passed it before retaking it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does ADI part 2 cost?',
    answer: `The test costs £${FEES.part2}. You pay again for each attempt.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use my own car for the ADI part 2 test?',
    answer: 'Yes. Your own car does not need dual controls, but it must meet GOV.UK\'s rules: no warning lights, legal tyres, an extra interior rear-view mirror and a passenger seatbelt and head restraint for the examiner, 4 wheels, and a top speed of at least 62mph with an mph speedometer. A hire car must have dual controls and meet all the other rules. If the car does not meet the rules the test is cancelled and you pay again.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I take ADI part 2 in an automatic?',
    answer: 'Yes, but the licence you hold decides it. With a manual licence you can take the test in a manual or an automatic car. With an automatic licence you must use an automatic.',
  },
